-- Q1: easy / L2 — Voluntary revocation consent threshold
(3, 'S Corporations', $EXPL$Ridgewood Corp, an S corporation, has four equal shareholders. Two shareholders want to revoke the S election, and two want to retain it. What is the minimum shareholder consent required to voluntarily revoke an S corporation election?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Shareholders holding more than 50% of the outstanding shares must consent", "Unanimous consent of all shareholders is required", "A simple majority of the number of shareholders must vote in favor", "Any single shareholder may unilaterally revoke the election"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 1362(d)(1), a voluntary revocation requires the consent of shareholders holding more than 50% of the shares of stock. The test is based on shares held, not head count. Wrong (B): Unanimous consent is required for the initial S election on Form 2553, not for revocation. Wrong (C): The revocation threshold is based on percentage of shares held, not a head count of shareholders. Wrong (D): No single shareholder can unilaterally revoke the election; more than 50% of shares must consent.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q2: medium / L2 — Family aggregation rule for 100-shareholder limit
(3, 'S Corporations', $EXPL$Brighton Inc., an S corporation, has 90 unrelated individual shareholders. The Sullivan family — consisting of a grandmother, her two adult children, and their seven grandchildren — also owns shares individually. For purposes of the 100-shareholder limit under Section 1361, how many total shareholders does Brighton have?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["100, because each family member counts individually", "91, because the Sullivan family counts as one shareholder under the family aggregation rule", "92, because only lineal descendants count as one and the grandmother is separate", "97, because only members of the same generation are aggregated"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 1361(c)(1), members of a family within six generations of a common ancestor are treated as a single shareholder. The ten Sullivan family members span three generations and are treated as one shareholder, giving Brighton 90 + 1 = 91 shareholders. Wrong (A): This ignores the family aggregation rule, which allows multi-generation families to count as one. Wrong (C): The rule aggregates all family members within six generations, not just lineal descendants. Wrong (D): The aggregation is not limited to same-generation members; it spans up to six generations from a common ancestor.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q3: medium / L2 — Partnership as ineligible S corporation shareholder
(3, 'S Corporations', $EXPL$Granite Corp, an S corporation with 40 individual shareholders, is approached by Apex Partners LP, a domestic limited partnership, which wants to purchase 10% of the outstanding shares. What is the result if Apex Partners acquires the stock?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The S election continues because domestic partnerships are eligible shareholders", "The S election continues if Apex Partners has fewer than 60 members", "The S election terminates because a partnership is not an eligible S corporation shareholder", "The S election continues if Apex Partners elects to be treated as a disregarded entity"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 1361(b)(1)(B), only individuals, estates, certain trusts, and certain tax-exempt organizations may be S corporation shareholders. Partnerships are categorically excluded, and acquiring stock would violate the eligibility requirements and terminate the S election. Wrong (A): Domestic status does not make a partnership eligible; partnerships are an excluded shareholder type. Wrong (B): The number of partners is irrelevant because no partnership may be an S corporation shareholder. Wrong (D): A limited partnership cannot elect disregarded entity status, and the partnership form is categorically ineligible.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q4: medium / L2 — Per-share, per-day allocation for mid-year ownership
(3, 'S Corporations', $EXPL$Oakmont Corp, a calendar-year S corporation with 100 shares outstanding, reports $73,000 of ordinary business income for 2026. Shareholder Hayes owns 50 shares for the entire year. Shareholder Voss owns 50 shares from January 1 through September 30 (273 days), then sells all shares to Shareholder Nolan on October 1. Under the default allocation method, what is Nolan's share of ordinary income?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$18,250, reflecting a half-year share of income", "$12,167, reflecting one-third of a half share of income", "$36,500, reflecting a full 50% share for the year", "$9,200, prorated based on per-share, per-day ownership"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 1377(a)(1), S corporation items are allocated on a per-share, per-day basis. Nolan owned 50 of 100 shares for 92 of 365 days: $73,000 x (50/100) x (92/365) = $9,200. Wrong (A): This assumes Nolan held shares for half the year (183 days), but Nolan held for only 92 days. Wrong (B): This uses a rough quarterly fraction rather than the required daily proration. Wrong (C): This ignores the proration requirement and allocates a full 50% share to Nolan despite partial-year ownership.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q5: hard / L3 — Exceeding the 100-shareholder limit terminates S election
(3, 'S Corporations', $EXPL$Ironwood Corp, an S corporation, has 99 unrelated individual shareholders. On March 1, 2026, two additional unrelated individuals each purchase newly issued shares from the corporation. What is the effect on Ironwood's S election?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The S election terminates because the shareholder count of 101 exceeds the 100-shareholder limit", "The S election continues because the limit is 150 shareholders, not 100", "The S election continues because the two new shareholders can be aggregated as one under the family rule", "The S election is suspended for 60 days while the corporation reduces its shareholder count"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 1361(b)(1)(A), an S corporation may not have more than 100 shareholders. Adding two unrelated individuals to the existing 99 brings the count to 101, which violates the limit and terminates the S election. Wrong (B): The statutory limit is 100 shareholders, not 150; there is no higher threshold. Wrong (C): The family aggregation rule under Section 1361(c)(1) applies only to members of a family within six generations of a common ancestor — unrelated individuals cannot be aggregated. Wrong (D): There is no suspension or cure period; the S election terminates immediately when an eligibility requirement is violated.$EXPL$, 'hard', 3),

-- Q6: medium / L3 — Computing ordinary business income (excluding separately stated items)
(3, 'S Corporations', $EXPL$Westbrook Corp, an S corporation, has the following 2026 results: gross revenues of $400,000, cost of goods sold of $180,000, operating expenses of $90,000, a $15,000 short-term capital gain, and a $10,000 charitable contribution. What is Westbrook's ordinary business income reported on Form 1120-S?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$105,000", "$130,000", "$145,000", "$120,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Ordinary business income on Form 1120-S equals gross revenue minus cost of goods sold and operating expenses: $400,000 - $180,000 - $90,000 = $130,000. The $15,000 short-term capital gain and $10,000 charitable contribution are separately stated items on Schedule K, excluded from ordinary business income. Wrong (A): This deducts the charitable contribution from ordinary income, but charitable contributions must be separately stated under Section 1366(a)(1)(A). Wrong (C): This adds the capital gain to ordinary income, but capital gains are separately stated items. Wrong (D): This inconsistently deducts the contribution but includes the capital gain.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q7: easy / L1 — Maximum number of S corporation shareholders
(3, 'S Corporations', $EXPL$What is the maximum number of shareholders an S corporation may have?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["50 shareholders", "75 shareholders", "100 shareholders", "Unlimited shareholders"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 1361(b)(1)(A), an S corporation may not have more than 100 shareholders. Members of a family within six generations of a common ancestor are counted as one shareholder. Wrong (A): 50 was the pre-2005 threshold, not the current limit — Congress raised it to 100. Wrong (B): 75 was an interim historical cap subsequently raised; it is no longer applicable. Wrong (D): Unlike C corporations and partnerships, which have no cap, S corporations are limited to 100 shareholders.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q8: medium / L2 — Nonresident alien shareholder terminates S election
(3, 'S Corporations', $EXPL$Harbor Corp, a calendar-year S corporation, has 30 individual shareholders who are all U.S. citizens. On July 1, 2026, Shareholder Kent sells his shares to Nakamura, a citizen and resident of Japan who is a nonresident alien for U.S. tax purposes. What is the effect on Harbor's S election?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The S election continues because the 100-shareholder limit is not exceeded", "The S election continues provided Nakamura files a U.S. income tax return", "The S election is suspended until Nakamura disposes of the shares", "The S election terminates because nonresident aliens are not eligible S corporation shareholders"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 1361(b)(1)(C), all S corporation shareholders must be U.S. citizens or resident aliens, estates, certain trusts, or certain tax-exempt organizations. A nonresident alien is categorically ineligible, and the acquisition terminates the S election. Wrong (A): Meeting the shareholder count limit is irrelevant when an ineligible shareholder acquires stock. Wrong (B): Filing a U.S. return does not convert a nonresident alien into an eligible S corporation shareholder. Wrong (C): There is no suspension mechanism for S elections; the election terminates on the date the ineligible shareholder acquires shares.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q9: easy / L1 — Identifying eligible S corporation shareholder types
(3, 'S Corporations', $EXPL$Which of the following is an eligible S corporation shareholder?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A qualified Subchapter S trust (QSST)", "A domestic C corporation", "A general partnership", "A nonresident alien individual"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 1361(d)(1), a qualified Subchapter S trust is an eligible S corporation shareholder provided the beneficiary makes the required election. QSSTs, along with grantor trusts and ESBTs, are specifically permitted. Wrong (B): Corporations, including C corporations, are not eligible S corporation shareholders under Section 1361(b)(1)(B). Wrong (C): Partnerships are categorically excluded from S corporation ownership under Section 1361(b)(1)(B). Wrong (D): Nonresident aliens are ineligible under Section 1361(b)(1)(C); only U.S. citizens and resident aliens qualify as individual shareholders.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q10: hard / L4 — Distributions reduce stock basis only, not debt basis
(3, 'S Corporations', $EXPL$Merritt, the sole shareholder of Sycamore Corp (an S corporation with no accumulated E&P), has stock basis of $10,000 and debt basis of $30,000 from a direct loan. During 2026, Sycamore reports $5,000 of ordinary income and distributes $20,000 cash to Merritt. Evaluate Merritt's ending stock basis, debt basis, and the tax treatment of the distribution.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Stock basis $0; debt basis $25,000; no taxable gain on the distribution", "Stock basis $0; debt basis $30,000; the $5,000 excess distribution is capital gain", "Stock basis $0; debt basis $30,000; no taxable gain because combined basis exceeds the distribution", "Stock basis $5,000; debt basis $30,000; no taxable gain on the distribution"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 1367, stock basis increases for income ($10,000 + $5,000 = $15,000), then decreases for the $20,000 distribution. Stock basis is reduced to $0 (not below zero), and the $5,000 excess is capital gain under Section 1368(b)(2). Distributions cannot reduce debt basis — only pass-through losses can. Debt basis remains $30,000. Wrong (A): This applies the excess $5,000 against debt basis, but distributions reduce only stock basis, not debt basis. Wrong (C): This assumes combined stock and debt basis can absorb the distribution, but distributions apply solely against stock basis. Wrong (D): This applies the distribution before income, violating the ordering rule that requires income items to increase basis first.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — Foreign taxes as a separately stated item on K-1
(3, 'S Corporations', $EXPL$Birchwood Corp, an S corporation, incurs $12,000 of foreign income taxes during 2026. Its sole shareholder, Farrell, needs to determine how the foreign taxes will appear on her Schedule K-1. How are the foreign taxes reported?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Deducted in computing Birchwood's ordinary business income on Form 1120-S", "Reported as an ordinary business expense reducing the shareholder's distributive share", "Separately stated on Schedule K-1 so the shareholder can elect to claim a credit or deduction", "Not reported to shareholders because only the S corporation claims the foreign tax benefit"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 1366(a)(1)(A), foreign taxes paid are a separately stated item because the shareholder must individually elect whether to claim a foreign tax credit or deduction on their personal return. Wrong (A): Foreign taxes cannot be netted into ordinary business income because doing so would eliminate the shareholder's individual credit-versus-deduction election. Wrong (B): This merges the item into ordinary business expenses, which would deprive the shareholder of the credit-versus-deduction choice. Wrong (D): Foreign taxes paid by an S corporation pass through to shareholders on Schedule K-1; the entity does not claim the benefit itself.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q12: easy / L1 — S corporation pass-through taxation (no entity-level tax)
(3, 'S Corporations', $EXPL$How is an S corporation's income generally taxed at the federal level?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The corporation pays tax at the 21% flat corporate rate on all taxable income", "The corporation pays tax at graduated rates based on income brackets", "The corporation withholds tax on distributions paid to shareholders", "The corporation does not pay entity-level tax; income passes through to shareholders"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 1363(a), an S corporation is generally not subject to entity-level federal income tax. Instead, income, deductions, and credits pass through to shareholders, who report them on their individual returns via Schedule K-1. Wrong (A): The 21% flat rate applies to C corporations under Section 11, not S corporations, which are pass-through entities. Wrong (B): Graduated corporate rates were eliminated, and unlike C corporations, S corporations do not pay entity-level income tax. Wrong (C): S corporations do not withhold tax on distributions; rather, shareholders report pass-through items directly on their individual returns.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q13: medium / L3 — Rescission of a voluntary revocation before effective date
(3, 'S Corporations', $EXPL$On October 15, 2026, shareholders holding 60% of Crestline Corp's stock (a calendar-year S corporation) file a revocation of the S election without specifying a prospective effective date. On December 1, 2026, the same shareholders want to undo the revocation before it takes effect. Can the revocation be rescinded?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Yes, because the revocation is not effective until January 1, 2027, and may be rescinded before that date with the consent of shareholders holding more than 50% of shares", "No, once filed with the IRS a revocation of S status is irrevocable", "Yes, but only if all shareholders unanimously consent to the rescission", "No, because the revocation became effective on October 15, 2026, the date it was filed"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 1362(d)(1)(C), a revocation made after the 15th day of the 3rd month is effective for the following tax year (January 1, 2027). The revocation may be rescinded before the effective date with the consent of shareholders holding more than 50% of shares. Wrong (B): Revocations are not irrevocable; they can be rescinded any time before the effective date with the required shareholder consent. Wrong (C): Rescission requires the same more-than-50% consent threshold as the revocation itself, not unanimous consent. Wrong (D): A revocation filed after March 15 does not take effect on the filing date; it defaults to January 1 of the following year.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q14: hard / L4 — Multi-factor S corporation eligibility evaluation
(3, 'S Corporations', $EXPL$Pinnacle Corp is a domestic corporation formed in Delaware with 85 individual shareholders, all U.S. citizens, including a married couple counted separately. The corporation has one class of common stock with equal distribution and liquidation rights but has issued both voting and nonvoting shares. Pinnacle is not a financial institution or insurance company. Evaluate whether Pinnacle is eligible to elect S corporation status.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Ineligible because issuing voting and nonvoting shares creates a prohibited second class of stock", "Eligible because all requirements are met — differences in voting rights alone do not create a second class of stock", "Ineligible because the married couple must be counted as one shareholder, altering the eligibility analysis", "Ineligible because a Delaware corporation is organized under state law and does not qualify as a domestic corporation"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Pinnacle meets all requirements under IRC Section 1361(b): it is a domestic corporation, has fewer than 100 eligible shareholders (all U.S. citizen individuals), has one class of stock (voting differences are permitted under Section 1361(c)(4)), and is not an ineligible type. Wrong (A): Under Section 1361(c)(4), differences in voting rights alone do not create a second class of stock; only differences in distribution or liquidation rights violate the requirement. Wrong (C): While a married couple may be aggregated as one under the family rule, separate counting does not make the entity ineligible — it only affects whether the count approaches 100. Wrong (D): A corporation organized under any U.S. state's law is a domestic corporation for federal tax purposes.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q15: easy / L1 — Unanimous consent required for initial S election
(3, 'S Corporations', $EXPL$What is required for a corporation's initial S corporation election to be valid?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Consent of shareholders holding more than 50% of shares", "Approval by the corporation's board of directors only", "Consent of all shareholders of the corporation", "A majority vote of the shareholders at an annual meeting"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 1362(a)(2), all shareholders must consent to the S election by signing Form 2553. Even one nonconsenting shareholder prevents the election from being valid. Wrong (A): The more-than-50% threshold applies to revoking an existing S election, not to making the initial election. Wrong (B): Board approval alone is insufficient; every shareholder must individually consent on Form 2553. Wrong (D): The S election is not made by majority vote; it requires the unanimous consent of all shareholders.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q16: medium / L3 — Third-party loan guarantee does NOT create debt basis
(3, 'S Corporations', $EXPL$Hanover Corp, an S corporation, borrows $200,000 from First National Bank. Shareholder Owens, the sole owner, personally guarantees the entire loan. Owens has stock basis of $40,000 and no direct loans to Hanover. Hanover reports an ordinary loss of $100,000, all allocated to Owens. How much of the loss can Owens deduct against basis?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The full $100,000 loss is deductible because the personal guarantee provides basis", "Owens may deduct $200,000 because the guaranteed loan creates debt basis for the full amount", "No portion of the loss is deductible because S corporation losses never pass through to shareholders", "Only $40,000 is deductible because a personal guarantee of a third-party loan does not create debt basis"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 1366(d)(1), S corporation losses are deductible only to the extent of the shareholder's stock basis plus debt basis. Debt basis requires a direct loan from the shareholder to the corporation. A third-party bank loan personally guaranteed by the shareholder does not create debt basis. Owens can deduct only $40,000 (stock basis). Wrong (A): A personal guarantee is not a direct loan and does not create debt basis for S corporation purposes. Wrong (B): The guarantee does not establish any debt basis regardless of the loan amount. Wrong (C): S corporation losses are deductible to the extent of the shareholder's available stock and debt basis.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q17: hard / L4 — Revocation effective date analysis (filed after 2.5-month window)
(3, 'S Corporations', $EXPL$Clarkson and Meyer are equal shareholders of Northstar Corp, a calendar-year S corporation. On June 1, 2026, both shareholders consent to revoke the S election without specifying a prospective effective date. Northstar has $120,000 of ordinary income for the full year 2026. Evaluate the S election status for 2026 and the resulting tax treatment of income.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Northstar remains an S corporation for all of 2026 and each shareholder reports $60,000 of pass-through income, because the revocation is effective January 1, 2027", "Northstar is an S corporation through May 31 and a C corporation starting June 1, splitting the year into two short periods", "The S election terminates immediately on June 1, and the full $120,000 is taxed at the corporate level for 2026", "The revocation is invalid because both shareholders must file separate revocation statements with their individual returns"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 1362(d)(1)(C)(i), a revocation made after the 15th day of the 3rd month (March 15 for calendar-year corporations) without specifying a prospective date is effective on the first day of the following tax year — January 1, 2027. Northstar remains an S corporation for all of 2026, and each equal shareholder reports $60,000. Wrong (B): A mid-year split occurs only when a specific prospective effective date is designated after the revocation date; absent such specification, a late revocation defaults to the next year. Wrong (C): The election does not terminate on the filing date when no prospective date is specified and the filing is after March 15. Wrong (D): A single revocation statement signed by shareholders holding more than 50% is sufficient; separate filings are not required.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q18: medium / L3 — Tax-exempt income does NOT increase AAA
(3, 'S Corporations', $EXPL$Briarwood Corp, an S corporation, reports $100,000 of ordinary business income and $8,000 of tax-exempt municipal bond interest for 2026. Beginning AAA is $25,000, and no distributions are made. What is Briarwood's ending AAA balance?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$133,000", "$125,000", "$100,000", "$108,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 1368(e)(1)(A), AAA is increased by ordinary income and separately stated income items but is not increased by tax-exempt income. Beginning AAA of $25,000 plus $100,000 ordinary income equals $125,000. The $8,000 tax-exempt interest is tracked separately, not in AAA. Wrong (A): This incorrectly adds tax-exempt income to AAA ($25,000 + $100,000 + $8,000 = $133,000), but tax-exempt items do not increase AAA. Wrong (C): This ignores the $25,000 beginning AAA balance and includes only current-year ordinary income. Wrong (D): This appears to add tax-exempt income to ordinary income while omitting the beginning balance.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q19: medium / L2 — New shareholder does NOT need to consent to existing S election
(3, 'S Corporations', $EXPL$Trident Corp has been an S corporation since 2020 with the consent of all original shareholders. In 2026, Shareholder Park sells her shares to Rivera, who has never owned stock in Trident. Must Rivera consent to the existing S election for it to continue?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Yes, Rivera must file a new Form 2553 within 75 days of acquiring the shares", "Yes, Rivera must consent within the first 2.5 months of the next tax year", "No, a new shareholder is not required to consent to an existing S election", "No, but Rivera must file a written acknowledgment with the IRS confirming awareness of the election"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 1362(a), shareholder consent is required only when the S election is initially made. A subsequent purchaser of stock takes the shares subject to the existing S election and need not consent for the election to continue. Wrong (A): Form 2553 is filed only for the initial election or a new election; it is not required for stock transfers to new shareholders. Wrong (B): The 2.5-month consent window applies to the initial election timing, not to subsequent stock purchases. Wrong (D): No written acknowledgment or separate filing is required from a new shareholder for the existing election to remain in effect.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q20: easy / L1 — Domestic corporation requirement for S election
(3, 'S Corporations', $EXPL$Which of the following is a requirement for an entity to qualify for S corporation status?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The entity must have at least two shareholders", "The entity must be publicly traded on a U.S. stock exchange", "The entity must be organized as a limited liability company", "The entity must be a domestic corporation organized under U.S. law"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 1361(b)(1), an S corporation must be a domestic corporation — one created or organized in the United States under federal or state law. Foreign corporations are ineligible for S status. Wrong (A): An S corporation may have a single shareholder; there is no minimum shareholder requirement. Wrong (B): Public trading is not required; most S corporations are closely held private companies. Wrong (C): An LLC is not a corporation by default and must first elect corporate classification before it can elect S corporation status.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1)
;

